34,2,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"My findings are concerning. We are losing half of our internal system knowledge this year, and hiring replacements is proving nearly impossible. Furthermore, past rollouts show that standard training is often ignored in favor of old habits.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Oh man, it's even worse than I thought! Our yard gear is a total mix of old stuff, and that 2021 trial was a disaster—people just went back to spreadsheets! Plus, we have zero downtime in winter.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"My findings confirm my suspicions. Capital requests are being trimmed, while recurring operating costs pass without scrutiny. Furthermore, bundled agreements stay stable, but separate add-ons have climbed yearly. We are bleeding money through hidden, recurring costs.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"Oh no, that's really worrying! I found out that during last winter's outage, nobody took charge for two whole days because the support was split up. Plus, our warehouse rules changed twice in one year! We really need a clear owner.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,It seems we are all uncovering a rather consistent theme of systemic fragility. My findings reveal a dispatch tool that has aged in place since 2012 and a 2018 data migration that left a trail of errors.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Yumi @Kenji, your findings reinforce my fears. If we have no clear support ownership and a history of ignoring training, the burden will fall entirely on a skeleton crew that is already disappearing. This is a recipe for burnout.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Yumi"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Misaki, exactly! And if the new system is too fancy for our old scanners, the crew will just go back to spreadsheets again. We can't afford another 2021 disaster or two days of paper dispatch!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Kenji @Misaki, the risk is not just technical or human, but financial. If we choose a vendor with fragmented licensing, those ""hidden"" costs will drain the budget meant for the very staffing and hardware you are worried about.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, you're so right! If the money disappears into hidden fees, we can't fix the support problems. We need a partner who takes full responsibility and can keep up with our changes without everything falling apart!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, your point on financial leakage is well-taken. It appears we are trapped in a cycle where we pay for ""solutions"" that are either left to rot or returned to us as broken puzzles. We must avoid another 2018.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, the ""broken puzzles"" are being solved by people who are no longer here or are too tired to care. We cannot prioritize a technical fix if the people implementing it are already at a breaking point.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Misaki, I totally get it! But if we get a system that's actually easy to use and works with our old gear, maybe the crew won't be so tired? We need something that just works!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Kenji, simplicity is a luxury that often comes with a high subscription price. If we prioritize ""ease of use"" without a fixed-cost structure, we will simply be trading technical frustration for a financial crisis in three years.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, @Kenji, you're both right! But can we find a vendor who offers both? I'm just so worried that if we don't have one clear person in charge, we'll be stuck in that two-day silence again!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, optimism is a virtue, but we must be realistic. We are looking for a partner, not a miracle worker. The pattern is clear: we outsource the effort and inherit the errors. We need a vendor who guarantees stability, not just a friendly face.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"Stability is meaningless if the internal team cannot maintain it. We must prioritize a vendor that provides extensive, hands-on implementation support to bridge our knowledge gap and prevent our remaining staff from collapsing.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I agree! We need a vendor who doesn't just drop a manual and leave. They have to understand that our yards are messy and our winter is crazy. If they can't handle that, they're not the right fit!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I agree that support is necessary, but it must be bundled. We cannot afford a vendor who charges separately for implementation and maintenance, as those costs will inevitably spiral and be ignored by the board.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Yumi,,"I totally agree! Let's find someone who takes full responsibility for everything—the costs, the support, and the changes. If we can get a partner who truly has our backs, I know we can make this work!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we have reached a rare consensus. We require a vendor that offers a comprehensive, bundled cost structure and a commitment to long-term stability over superficial features. Let us see which candidates actually meet these criteria.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
